# Optimization Log: matmul_24576_768_384
# Shape: (24576×768) @ (768×384) → f64
# Date: 2026-02-26 15:57
# Hardware: Intel Xeon E5-2680 v4 (Broadwell), AVX2, 28 cores, L1=32KB

## Problem Analysis
- M=24576 (very large, 2^13 × 3), K=768, N=384
- f64: AVX2 = 4-wide vectors
- M >> N: prioritize M-dimension parallelism
- Clean M divisors: 24, 32, 48, 96, 128, 256, 384, 512, 768, 1024, 1536, 2048...
- Clean N divisors: 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64, 96, 128, 192, 384

## Strategy
- Apply forall (M-heavy threads) + tile_K + tile_reg + vectorize + unroll_K
- Based on: 256_512_1024 best was [4,8]+K64+[4,8,16]; 512_512_512 best was [4,4]+K32+[4,8,8]
- For large M, N-smaller shape: try M-heavy configs like [16,2], [24,1], [32,1]

---

## Step 0: Baselines

- MLIR baseline: 17515.725 ms
- PyTorch: 18.856 ms

## Candidate 1: forall[32×M: tile=768] + K[64] + [4,8,8] + vectorize + unroll×4
- Time: 38.873 ms | Speedup: 450.6× | vs PyTorch: 0.485× (2.06× slower)

## Candidate 2: forall[24×M: tile=1024] + K[64] + [4,8,8] + vectorize + unroll×4
- Time: 27.839 ms | *** BEST SO FAR *** | vs PyTorch: 0.677× (1.48× slower)
- Key insight: 24 threads = largest clean divisor ≤ 28 cores → perfect load balance
  With 24 threads on 28 cores: each of 24 active cores processes M=1024 rows exactly once
  With 32 threads on 28 cores: some cores handle 2 work items → load imbalance at end

## Candidate 3: forall[16×M: tile=1536] + K[64] + [4,8,8] + vectorize + unroll×4
- Time: 41.885 ms | WORSE than 24-thread config
- Too few threads: 16/28 = 57% core utilization

### Thread sweep summary: 24 >> 32 > 16
### Next: K tile size tuning on forall[24×M]


## Candidate 4: forall[24×M: tile=1024] + K[32] + [4,8,8] + vectorize + unroll×4
- Time: 34.816 ms | WORSE — K=32 gives 2× more outer iterations with no cache benefit

## Candidate 5: forall[24×M: tile=1024] + K[64] + [4,8,16] + vectorize + unroll×4
- Vectors: vector<4x8x16xf64> = 512 elements (at limit)
- Time: 27.469 ms | Marginal improvement, within noise of C2

## Candidate 6: forall[24×M: tile=1024] + K[64] + [4,8,8] + vectorize + unroll×8 (full)
- Full unroll of 8 K_inner iterations
- Time: 27.348 ms | *** BEST *** — marginally better, essentially same as C2/C5

### K tile and inner tile summary:
- K=64 optimal (B_K_slice=196KB fits in L2); K=32 loses (2× overhead), K=128 not tested (would spill L2)
- Inner tile [4,8,8] vs [4,8,16] vs unroll factor: all within 2% — no significant difference
- K=64 with any of {[4,8,8]×4, [4,8,16]×4, [4,8,8]×8} gives ~27.35-27.84ms

## Candidate 7 (failed): forall num_threads[28] + K[64] + [4,8,8] + vectorize
- num_threads [28] fails: non-uniform tiles (877/878 rows) → vectorize fails
- 24576 not divisible by 28 → cannot use all 28 cores cleanly

## Candidate 8: forall[6,4] (24 threads, M=4096, N=96) + K[64] + [4,8,8] + vectorize + unroll×4
- 2D parallelism: B_K_slice = 64×96×8 = 49KB (well under L2 vs 196KB with [24,1])
- Time: 35.989 ms | WORSE — N-parallelism on small N=384 causes load imbalance (24 items unequally on 28 cores) and smaller N_tile reduces sequential work per item

